          "type": "output_text",
          "text": "## Finding\n\nYes. One cost event for the September manufacturing order ending in **1070** was created after the September cost period entered Pending Close.\n\n- **Work order:** `WO-2025-09-004` — `MO-25-09-1070`\n- **Cost event:** `CE-250904-0004`\n- **Event type:** `INPUT`\n- **Source transaction:** `TXN-ISSUE-250930-1070`\n- **Amount:** **28,476,342 cents** (**$284,763.42**)\n- **Exact Pending Close time:** `2025-09-30T18:00:00`\n- **Exact event-created time:** `2025-09-30T19:15:00`\n- **Lateness:** **75 minutes** (4,500 seconds)\n- **Recorded accounting status:** `POSTED`\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe `POSTED` status is **not consistent** with Oracle Fusion Cloud SCM Cost Management 26B’s documented Pending Close treatment. Oracle states that transactions created after the Pending Close date—even when related to the current or a prior period—aren’t processed until the period is closed or reopened. This was an `INPUT` transaction, not one of the documented exceptions such as revenue lines, COGS recognition, or manual cost adjustments. Therefore, its recorded `POSTED` status is inconsistent with the policy.\n\nThe review was read-only; no database records were changed. The audit result and provenance were saved to `/cache/work/worker_85c0bb4450b2/audit-report.json`.",
